  "surprise": "P50-family x P01 recurrent MTP (the 136 pretraining-only MTP module re-applied to its own output with shared weights, predicting t+3 after t+2, each stage at weight 0.3; handoff model identical to 136's 4,800,000-param architecture) on 136-fable scored 651/650/621 on seeds 0/1/2 vs 136's 655/673/646: exact means 640.67 vs 658.00, -17.33, paired -4/-23/-25, every seed below its counterpart, inside the registered -40..+35 range and below its central +5. Discard. Pretraining cost more than predicted: 2869/2880/2829 steps (136: 3370/3351/3367, -15%, below the predicted 2,950-3,250; 2.4 epochs), final main val loss 0.0775/0.0894/0.0840 (136: 0.086/0.083/0.079; minima 0.0775/0.0777/0.0764, so seeds 1-2 ended on an upswing of the noisy val curve), train loss 0.054/0.055/0.057 (136 ~0.055), held-out greedy 0.9725/0.9615/0.970 (136: 0.965/0.9705/0.968; inside the predicted 0.955-0.975). Surprise 1: the t+3 loss ended at 0.0546/0.0558/0.0560, within 0.001 of the t+2 loss (0.0541/0.0554/0.0554) and equal to the main next-token loss, not the predicted 0.01-0.03 above it: in this Lean surface form the token three ahead is as predictable as the next one, so the second stage is not a harder planning target, just a third copy of full-strength next-token-like supervision, bought with 15% fewer steps. Surprise 2: the registered failure mode (a) happened at round 1 but NOT at round 4. The RL start was far weaker than 136's: round-1 targets solved 293/323/495 (136: 577/529/662; predicted 520-680), round-1 sample acc 0.076/0.083/0.133 (136: 0.137/0.135/0.210), whole-run Lean parse failures 62541/53217/45747 of 266,912 = 23.4/19.9/17.1% (136: 17.0%), both-ok samples 294,529 vs 314,358 (-6.3%). Expert iteration then climbed much faster (r1->r4 +841/+812/+597, mean +750 vs 136's +525) to round-4 1134/1135/1092 (mean 1120 vs 136's 1102/1114/1124 = 1113), i.e. the RL-solved count ended ABOVE the incumbent's, but the transfer from RL solves to the dev pool was worse: eval/round-4 ratio 0.574/0.573/0.569 vs 136's 0.594/0.604/0.575, the exact quantity 136 won on. Eval: L* 11/11/11 (136: 11/11/11), solved at >=10 93/96/95 (136: 94/110/104), >=11 20/21/20 (19/24/24), >=12 4/3/3 (4/4/3), seed 0 by-bin 93/122/343/73/16/4 for lengths 7-12 vs 136 seed 0 95/119/347/75/15/4 (a -4 tie), seed 2 lost at lengths 7-9 (91/102/333). Reading: a deeper recurrent MTP horizon reverses 136's pattern (136: weaker round 4, better transfer; 138: stronger round 4, worse transfer), which fits the extra stage acting as more next-token-shaped supervision that makes the trunk fit the RL target pool faster without generalising better, rather than as the planning signal registered; with 136's +12 and this -17 each about one 3-seed sd, the safe conclusion is that the second horizon does not help and costs 15% of the steps, not that it is harmful. The shared module handled both input distributions (failure mode (b) did not occur: the t+3 loss tracked the t+2 loss from 60 s on). Follow-ups: the MTP weight (0.1 / 1.0 at depth 1) is the cheaper lever now that every horizon's loss equals the main loss; a depth-2 variant only makes sense if the second stage is made nearly free (e.g. proj + head without the block) so the step count is preserved. Sampling cost unchanged as predicted: totals 1487/1468/1571 s (136: 1517/1549/1616). CPU checks before launch: main loss identical to the fork's loss_on (4.761792 both), gradients on every MTP and trunk parameter, perturbing x[7] changes stage-1 outputs only from position 6 and stage-2 outputs only from position 5 (no leakage into any position predicting x[7]), cached vs full forward max abs diff 9.1e-7, model.n_params() == 4,800,000. Registration bcc9801 committed before pods; all three pods finished ok (27/27/28 min), run_idea.py exited 0 after terminating them (pod list shows no autoresearch pods). Candidate added 26 lines and deleted 14; discard restores the incumbent.",
